  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA weak cold front will track across the region tonight, with a few \nshowers possible from the NY/PA line north. The potential for \nshowers will increase with northward extent with the I90 corridor \nreceiving a few hundredths to a tenth of an inch of rainfall. Shower \npotential will be better in these areas where there is closer \nproximity to a low level jet crossing the area, helping to increase \nforcing some. Otherwise the most notable feature with the cold \nfrontal passage will be increased clouds and cooler temperatures in \nthe mid 50s to low 60s for Friday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nBehind the passing cold front tonight, a cooler airmass will push \ninto the region, with lingering lake clouds through the day on \nFriday. High pressure will track from the Upper Midwest, across the \nGreat Lakes and to the coast of New England from Friday through \nSunday, providing dry weather for the period. A couple of lake \nshowers can't be completely ruled out for Friday across northern \nportions of the area, but the dry nature of the airmass should limit \nthe shower potential. \n\nContinued ridging across the eastern portion of the CONUS through \nthe middle of next week will continue the mostly dry pattern. There \nis a chance that a weakening trough and a nearby passing warm front \nmay bring a couple of showers later Monday into Tuesday. \n\nTemperatures will be coolest for Friday and Saturday with afternoon \nhighs in the mid 50s to low 60s. From there temperatures warm to the \nupper 50s to upper 60s for Sunday through the middle of next week, \nwith some near 70 values also possible at times for next week. \n\nThe next potential for more meaningful rainfall is expected for late \nWednesday into Thursday of next week.",
